  "body": {
    "id": "thm:singleton-recovery",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every sequence \\(a_n\\to\\infty\\), uniformly over \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal K\\) with \\(\\mathcal P(\\Sigma)=\\{a\\}\\) and \\(\\delta_a(\\Sigma)\\ge a_n/\\sqrt n\\), \\(\\Pr_{\\Sigma}\\{\\Gamma_n=\\{a\\}\\}\\to1\\). More precisely, when \\(\\delta_a(\\Sigma)>2r_n\\) and \\(\\delta_a(\\Sigma)>2/n\\), the failure probability is at most \\(16V/(n\\delta_a(\\Sigma)^2)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "an",
      "n",
      "polarity",
      "Sigma",
      "K",
      "Pset",
      "deltapol",
      "Gamman",
      "rn",
      "Vconst"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:covariance-conditioning",
      "ass:gaussian-snapshots",
      "def:polarity-confidence-set",
      "thm:finite-sample-coverage"
    ],
    "justification": "Coverage becomes informative away from the opposing-sign frontier at the regular covariance-estimation scale.",
    "gap": "No cited OU paper proves uniform exact-set recovery; generic set inversion does not resolve the nearest-model branch when \\(B_n\\) is empty.",
    "consumer": "Analysts obtain a transparent clearance condition under which the honest set collapses to the identified polarity.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal K\\) with \\(\\mathcal P(\\Sigma)=\\{a\\}\\), write \\(\\delta=\\delta_a(\\Sigma)\\), and put \\(e=\\lVert S_n-\\Sigma\\rVert_F\\).  Suppose first that \\(0<\\delta<\\infty\\), \\(\\delta>2r_n\\), \\(\\delta>2/n\\), and \\(e<\\delta/4\\).  If \\(B_n\\ne\\varnothing\\), then every \\(\\Theta\\in B_n\\) satisfies\n\\[\n \\lVert\\Theta-\\Sigma\\rVert_F\n \\le\\lVert\\Theta-S_n\\rVert_F+e\n \\le r_n+e<3\\delta/4<\\delta. \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThus no such \\(\\Theta\\) belongs to \\(K_{-a}\\).  If \\(B_n=\\varnothing\\), the fallback and \\(\\rho(S_n)\\le e\\) give\n\\[\n \\lVert\\Theta_{j_n(S_n)}-\\Sigma\\rVert_F\n <\\rho(S_n)+1/n+e\\le2e+1/n<\\delta. \\tag{19}\n\\]\nAgain the selected model is not in \\(K_{-a}\\).  Every selected covariance is in \\(\\mathcal K\\), so its polarity set is nonempty; absence of \\(-a\\) makes it exactly \\(\\{a\\}\\).  Equations (18)--(19) therefore imply \\(\\Gamma_n=\\{a\\}\\).\n\nBy (16),\n\\[\n \\Pr_{\\Sigma}\\{e\\ge\\delta/4\\}\n \\le\\frac{16\\mathbb E_{\\Sigma}e^2}{\\delta^2}\n \\le\\frac{16V}{n\\delta^2}. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nThis is the stated finite-sample failure bound.  If \\(\\delta=\\infty\\), then \\(K_{-a}=\\varnothing\\); every covariance selected in either branch has polarity set \\(\\{a\\}\\), so recovery is certain.\n\nFinally, if \\(\\delta\\ge a_n/\\sqrt n\\) and \\(a_n\\to\\infty\\), then uniformly over the stated class the two strict conditions on \\(\\delta\\) hold for all large \\(n\\), because \\(a_n>2R\\) and \\(a_n\\sqrt n>2\\).  The right side of (20) is at most \\(16V/a_n^2\\to0\\).  This proves uniform singleton recovery.",
